Subject: cli: write a perf jitdump with --profiler jitdump

The CLI's --profiler option chose a perf map, or a GDB or LLDB command
file, all through the compiler configuration. A jitdump is chosen on the
engine instead, and the option had no value for it.

--profiler jitdump sets the engine's profile mode to JitDump wherever the
CLI builds a compiling engine, for every backend, and --profiler perfmap
now sets PerfMap there too, alongside the compiler setting it already
made; the GDB and LLDB values leave the engine's mode off. A jitdump is
written only on Linux, so the value is refused elsewhere, as
--experimental-artifact is.

A profile of a guest also needs the CLI's own frames, and perf's libdw
unwinder takes an executable's load bias to be its mapping's start minus
the mapping's file offset. lld places the CLI's executable segment 4 KiB
further into memory than into the file, so that bias comes out 4 KiB too
high and perf unwinds the CLI's frames with a neighbouring function's
rules. On Linux the build script links the wasmer binary with
-z separate-code, which gives executable code pages of its own at the
same offset in the file as in memory.

diff --git a/lib/cli/build.rs b/lib/cli/build.rs
index 6de63ed..5eb2e54 100644
--- a/lib/cli/build.rs
+++ b/lib/cli/build.rs
@@ -13,4 +13,13 @@ pub fn main() {
         println!("cargo:rustc-env=WASMER_BUILD_DATE={date}");
     }
     println!("cargo:rustc-env=WASMER_REPRODUCIBLE_BUILD={reproducible_build}");
+
+    if std::env::var("CARGO_CFG_TARGET_OS").as_deref() == Ok("linux") {
+        // perf's libdw unwinder takes the load bias to be the mapping's
+        // start minus its file offset, which is wrong for lld's default
+        // layout, so host PCs use another function's unwind rules. Put
+        // executable code in its own PT_LOAD segment at the same offset
+        // in the file as in memory.
+        println!("cargo:rustc-link-arg-bin=wasmer=-Wl,-z,separate-code");
+    }
 }
